<div class="lynk-post">

<div class="stats">
  <div class="stat"><span class="n">5,200</span><span class="l">automated orders per test run</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><span class="n">99.94%</span><span class="l">end-to-end completion rate</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><span class="n">11.8s → 0.26s</span><span class="l">purchase step execution time</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><span class="n">0</span><span class="l">lost or doubly-assigned units</span></div>
</div>

<p class="lede">
  A Murabaha order is not a row insert. It is a multi-step, multi-party sequence that must happen in exact
  order, because the order is what makes the trade legal. Load-test that sequence and MySQL does not slow
  down gracefully — it stops. This is how we went from <strong>four stuck orders out of ten</strong> to
  <strong>5,200 orders end to end at 99.94%</strong>, told as nine fights, ranked by what each one bought us.
</p>

<p>
  When you map a strictly ordered financial contract onto a relational database, you hit the physical limits
  of concurrency almost immediately. We had a mathematically correct state machine that guaranteed Sharia
  compliance. At 31 concurrent orders, it froze. The rigid consistency the contract demands is exactly what
  turned the database into the bottleneck.
</p>
<p>
  Each fight below is written the same way: the symptom we saw, the trap we fell into first, the pivot that
  reframed the problem, the fix, and the measured result. The traps are included on purpose — the wrong
  first guess is usually the most useful part of the story.
</p>

<div class="note">
  <strong>Background:</strong> if the contract itself is unfamiliar — why the steps cannot be reordered,
  why stock has to exist as individually coded units — that is covered separately in
  <a href="/blog/murabaha-as-a-state-machine/">Murabaha as a State Machine</a>. This article stands on its
  own; that one explains the machine being scaled.
</div>

